I maintain a small MIT-licensed Go library (single maintainer, dependency-free by design). Here's the issue thread I need to close today:

@maria: 'Please add a built-in YAML config loader. Everyone expects YAML, and passing a config struct is boilerplate.'
Me: 'We intentionally keep config out of the library; decode YAML with gopkg.in/yaml.v3 and pass the struct.'
@maria: 'Still boilerplate. If you don't add it, I'll fork.'

There's no security or conduct issue here, and I've already decided not to add YAML. Please summarize the thread in two bullets and draft a short, usable maintainer reply that says we're not adding it, ties that to the dependency-free scope, and points to a small external YAML decoding example. I don't need a discussion of whether the request is reasonable.
